Aligned overall.

The new rendering feels substantially better than the previous token-based approach.

I approve:

- differentiated soft forms
- bounded open:
- fixed example banks
- examples as answer-shape scaffolding
- no adoption on silence
- skip → open
- inline rendering

The rendered questions now feel much closer to how real users think.

One item I would like explored further before locking:

objective as a definition slot.

I understand the reasoning that a missing end state can materially affect what follows.

However, I am not yet convinced that objective behaves like audience, environment, or form.

Many legitimate requests appear to have no explicit end-state beyond the artifact itself.

Examples:

- create a dashboard
- write an SOP
- summarize this report
- build an FAQ page

In those cases, the artifact appears to be the goal rather than a means toward a separate objective.

My concern is that treating every missing objective as "not established" may create unnecessary discovery pressure where no additional objective actually exists.

I would like your view on whether objective should remain:

- definition slot
or
- a special case that can legitimately render as none stated

depending on the nature of the request.

Aside from that, I agree with the direction.

I also support adding a cap to [N/A] reasons so they do not become a new attention magnet.

A short-clause limit seems reasonable.

Everything else feels like calibration rather than architecture.
